    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok that makes sense. so basically it's just adding random data so the hashes dont match? i'm still a bit fuzzy on the rainbow table part though. how does adding that salt actually break the rainbow table attack specifically?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A rainbow table is essentially a massive, pre-computed lookup table of common passwords and their corresponding hashes. It's like a giant cheat sheet for hackers. If you don't use a salt, the attacker can just look up the hash they stole in their pre-built table and instantly find the original password. When you add a unique salt to each password, the input to the hashing function changes every single time, even if the password itself is the same. This means the attacker's pre-computed rainbow table becomes useless because the hash they need to look up is now unique to that specific user's salt.",
      "source": "target",
